he Ziegfeld Follies, which debuted in 1907, succeeded the European revue tradition by incorporating American themes and styles."}], "places": [{"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Theatre Capital"}, {"name": "London, England", "role": "Early Adoption"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: revue", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "revue"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q918727", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revue"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Revues shaped the landscape of theatrical entertainment, influencing the development of modern musical theatre and variety shows, with lasting impacts on global entertainment culture.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-30T13:10:16Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30545273640", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Theatrical entertainment that combines music, dance and sketches", "newValue": "Revues are theatrical performances that emerged prominently in the late 19th century, particularly in France, becoming a significant form of entertainment across Europe and eventually the world. Characterized by their blend of music, dance, and comedic sketches, revues have roots in earlier forms of entertainment such as vaudeville and operetta, with key figures like Georges Feydeau and Maurice Chevalier shaping their evolution. The Parisian cabaret scene, exemplified by venues like the Moulin Rouge, served as a crucible for the development of the revue, which not only reflected but also influenced the social and political dynamics of its time.

The first recognized revue is often credited to the Folies Bergère in 1869, where the format was refined to include a series of short acts that captivated audiences with their variety and spectacle. By the early 20th century, revues became synonymous with lavish productions that highlighted contemporary issues, often laced with satire and humor. The transition of the revue from Europe to America in the 1920s led to the creation of iconic Broadway shows, which further popularized this genre and brought about the rise of figures like Ziegfeld, who produced the famed Ziegfeld Follies.

